Here is the root cause:
The bug arises from the DSA framework's handling of device tree blobs that lack the `phy-mode` property. When this property is absent, the default PHY interface type is set to `PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_GMII`, which is not correctly interpreted as an internal PHY connection. This leads to compatibility issues when the driver expects a specific PHY interface mode to be defined, resulting in potential misconfigurations and failures in establishing the correct MAC connection to the internal PHY.

Here is the patch:

 static void ksz_phylink_get_caps(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port,
 				 struct phylink_config *config)
 {
 	struct ksz_device *dev = ds->priv;

 	config->legacy_pre_march2020 = false;

 	if (dev->info->supports_mii[port])
 		__set_bit(PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_MII, config->supported_interfaces);

 	if (dev->info->supports_rmii[port])
 		__set_bit(PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_RMII,
 			  config->supported_interfaces);

 	if (dev->info->supports_rgmii[port])
 		phy_interface_set_rgmii(config->supported_interfaces);

-	if (dev->info->internal_phy[port])
+	if (dev->info->internal_phy[port]) {
 		__set_bit(PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_INTERNAL,
 			  config->supported_interfaces);
+		/* Compatibility for phylib's default interface type when the
+		 * phy-mode property is absent
+		 */
+		__set_bit(PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_GMII,
+			  config->supported_interfaces);
+	}

 	if (dev->dev_ops->get_caps)
 		dev->dev_ops->get_caps(dev, port, config);
 }



